<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bangkok</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krung Thep</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bangkok is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, on the Chao Phraya river near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand">Gulf of Thailand</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> founded it as the capital of the Chakri dynasty in 1782 on Rattanakosin island, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>'s capital at Thonburi, and laid it out on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. It became the political, religious and commercial centre of Siam and the seat of modern Thailand's government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bangkok.jpg" alt="Bangkok" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:4Y1A1159_Bangkok_(33536795515).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Law drew on the Indian Dharmashastra tradition adapted to local custom, and much of Ayutthaya's legislation survives in the Three Seals Code compiled at <b>Bangkok</b> in 1805.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Konbaung wars reshaped mainland Southeast Asia: the destruction of Ayutthaya in 1767 cleared the way for the rise of Thonburi and <b>Bangkok</b> under Taksin and the Chakri dynasty, and the Burmese victories over the Qing are remembered as one of the most successful defences against Chinese invasion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its sacred images also played a part in later history: the Emerald Buddha, taken from Vientiane by Siamese forces in 1779, became the palladium of the Chakri kings in <b>Bangkok</b>, while the Phra Bang still gives Luang Prabang its name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bangkok</b> was laid out on the model of Ayutthaya, with moats, canals and walls built largely by conscripted Lao and Khmer labourers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sukhothai was a modest Tai kingdom that rose as Khmer power waned, but its patronage of Theravada Buddhism, its early Thai script and the idealised image of Ram Khamhaeng's paternal rule made it the founding kingdom of Thai national history and a model for Ayutthaya and <b>Bangkok</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He saw that the low-fired pots were old and their designs unique, and took samples to Princess Phanthip Chumbote at the Suan Pakkad Palace museum in <b>Bangkok</b> and to the Fine Arts Department; the art historian Elisabeth Lyons later sent sherds to the University of Pennsylvania for dating.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ban-chiang-culture">Ban Chiang culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
